menu "ac3forge hearth sink"

# Defaults chosen to be safe on an ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 rather than to match any
# particular DAC board: 5, 6 and 7 are ordinary GPIOs - not strapping pins
# (0, 45, 46), not the USB pair (19, 20), not the console UART (43, 44) and not
# the flash/PSRAM range. Any free GPIO works; I2S goes through the GPIO matrix.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_BCLK_GPIO
    int "I2S bit clock (BCLK/SCK) GPIO"
    default 5

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_WS_GPIO
    int "I2S word select (WS/LRCK) GPIO"
    default 6

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DOUT_GPIO
    int "I2S data out (DOUT/SDIN) GPIO"
    default 7

# A SECOND I2S LINE. Off by default: nothing about a normal build needs it.
# Line 0 above stays the clock master; line 1, when enabled, is a slave whose
# BCLK/WS inputs are routed - through the GPIO matrix, not an external wire -
# to the SAME pin numbers line 0 already drives as outputs, so the two lines'
# frames stay sample-aligned. Only its DATA pin is new; see sink/i2s/
# audio_sink.cpp for why a second line has to run the exact same frame shape
# as the first rather than whatever a narrower layout would need on its own.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE
    int "A second I2S line is wired (1) or not (0), for layouts wider than one line reaches"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        An int rather than a bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant even
        when it is off - see AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLAVE's own note on the same
        choice; a bool Kconfig option that is "n" emits no macro at all, only
        one that is "y" does. One ESP32-S3 I2S line carries at most 128 bits a
        frame - 4 slots at 32 bits (see AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLOT_BITS). A
        second, independent I2S peripheral doubles that ceiling, sharing line
        0's bit clock and word select so both stay aligned to the same frame.
        Needs a DAC or DSP that actually accepts two separate TDM lines - most
        simple stereo breakouts do not. Reconfiguring to bring up both lines'
        TDM mode has run on a DevKitC-1-N16R8 (GPIO matrix routing, mode
        switch, no crash) but not with anything on the wire to confirm the
        two lines stay sample-aligned - confirm that before trusting audio
        that depends on it. It is also two DMA buffers instead of one, each
        sized from AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_DESCRIPTORS x _DMA_FRAMES at this
        line's own ceiling width, in internal RAM: on the same board, over
        WiFi (AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP), reconfiguring to a six-channel
        unfolded layout with this on left too little contiguous internal RAM
        for the decode task's own stack, which failed to start - a real
        budget limit this project has hit, not a hang or a wrong answer, and
        worth sizing AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_DESCRIPTORS down before relying
        on this over a network source.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DOUT2_GPIO
    int "Second I2S line's data out GPIO"
    default 8
    help
        Only read when AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE is 1 - left visible
        otherwise for the same reason AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_BCLK_GPIO and
        friends are, regardless of which sink is even selected: one fewer
        Kconfig symbol that disappears from sdkconfig depending on another.

# THE SLOT WIDTH AND THE CLOCK ROLE.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLOT_BITS
    int "I2S slot width in bits (16 or 32)"
    default 32
    range 16 32
    help
        32-bit slots carry the decoder's 24-bit samples left-justified, which
        every DAC tried here accepts and a SigmaDSP requires, at twice the bit
        clock of 16 (3.07 MHz against 1.54 for stereo at 48 kHz). A TDM frame
        holds 128 bits, so one line carries four 32-bit slots or eight 16-bit
        ones: at 16 a 7.1 layout fits one line. With the second line
        (AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE) that doubles either way - sixteen
        channels at 16 bits, eight at 32 - so a 7.1.4 layout leaves the part
        only at 16 bits with both lines wired. The DMA queue is sized for the
        line's widest frame either way, eight 16-bit slots being the same 16
        bytes a frame as four 32-bit ones. Only 16 and 32 are valid.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLAVE
    int "The DAC supplies the clocks (1) or the ESP32-S3 does (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        0, master: the ESP32-S3 generates BCLK and WS from its own clock, which
        is what a DAC breakout wants. 1, slave: BCLK and WS are inputs on the
        same two pins, driven by the other end - how an ADAU1452 or ADAU1467
        that is the house's clock wants it; the sample rate is then what the
        peripheral is told to expect rather than what it makes. An int rather
        than a bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant.

# THE FRAME: whether a one- or two-channel layout opens standard I2S or the
# full TDM frame every other layout gets. See ac3forge/sink_plan.hpp.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_FIXED_FRAME
    int "Every layout runs the full TDM frame (1), or 1-2 channels open standard I2S (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        0: one or two channels open standard I2S, the two-slot frame a stereo
        I2S DAC such as a PCM5102 or MAX98357A reads, and three or more open
        TDM at the line's full width. 1: every layout opens that full TDM
        frame, eight 16-bit slots or four 32-bit ones, mono and stereo
        included, with the slots past the layout zeroed. A TDM DAC set up for
        one frame shape needs this: an ESS ES9080 has its slot count, slot
        width and channel map written over I2C, and its PLL can lock to the
        bit clock, so a stereo play opened as standard I2S would change the
        bit clock under it and put the samples in slots it does not read. A
        mono layout rides slot 0 alone; map two of the DAC's outputs to that
        slot to hear it on both. A one- or two-channel play then holds the DMA
        buffers of the full frame: 16 KB at the default depth where standard
        I2S's stereo pair at 16 bits holds 4 KB, which an ESP32-C6 playing 2.0
        showed as 12,304 bytes less free heap. With
        AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE, line 1
        also runs for every layout, its slots zeroed while line 0 holds the
        whole layout, so its DMA buffers stay allocated for every play (see
        that option's note on internal RAM). The DAC's own I2C setup is not
        part of this example. An int rather than a bool so it reaches C++ as a
        plain constant.

# THE I2S DMA QUEUE:how much audio the peripheral holds between the player's
# writes, as descriptors of this many sample frames each. Four of 240 is 960
# stereo 16-bit frames - 3,840 bytes, 20 ms at 48 kHz - which is LESS than one
# coded frame (1,536 samples, 6,144 bytes). A write only completes as the DAC
# drains, so once playback is under way the producer has the queue's depth, not
# the frame's 32 ms, between one write returning and the DAC running dry.
#
# Small on purpose for the flash and SD sources: every millisecond here is a
# millisecond of latency, and a deep queue would hide a decoder that cannot
# keep up, which is one of the things this example exists to reveal. A network
# source is a different case, and this is where its depth is set - the README
# records what a board needed.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_DESCRIPTORS
    int "I2S DMA descriptors"
    default 4
    range 2 64
    help
        With AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_FRAMES, how much audio the DMA queue
        holds: the default is about 21 ms. The player hands the sink a frame's
        six blocks together, 32 ms, and a 7.1.4 stream folded to 2.0 from a
        local source needs the queue to hold them and cover the next frame's
        decode too: twelve descriptors of 256 frames, 64 ms, for 16 KB more of
        internal SRAM. On a DevKitC-1 with no PSRAM, 714-tones.ec3 from the
        partition had 251 of 1,512 blocks reach an empty queue at the default,
        and none at twelve.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_FRAMES
    int "Sample frames per I2S DMA descriptor"
    default 240
    range 8 1023
    help
        1023 is the driver's ceiling for stereo 16-bit: a descriptor holds at
        most 4,092 bytes. The sinks use the largest descriptor at or under this
        that divides the player's 256-frame block, and add descriptors to keep
        the depth: a write that ends part-way through a descriptor can have the
        rest of it played as silence (see sink/sink_common.hpp).

# WHICH SINK. Kconfig rather than a -D cache variable, because the component's
# own CMakeLists is evaluated in IDF's separate requirements-expansion process,
# which does not inherit cache variables set by the project above it - CONFIG_
# values it does see. It also means CI selects the sink through the
# sdkconfig.ci it already overlays, rather than through a second mechanism.
#
# Read from CMake (main/CMakeLists.txt picks a directory), never from C++: the
# player does not know which sink it has, which is the point of the seam.
choice AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK
    prompt "Audio sink"
    default AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_I2S
    help
        Where decoded audio goes. The I2S sink reconfigures its own mode and
        slot count to whatever a layout needs, standard I2S for one or two
        speakers or TDM beyond that, or TDM for every layout with
        AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_FIXED_FRAME (ac3forge/sink_plan.hpp), so a layout
        change over the control surface never needs a rebuild. The capture
        sink is what CI runs: qemu-system-xtensa has no I2S peripheral, so a
        real write blocks on a DMA that never drains. It converts with the
        same code (ac3forge/interleave.hpp) and checks the result, with no
        peripheral behind it and no hardware ceiling to refuse against, so
        the source, the access-unit framing and the decode run as they do on
        a board for layouts a real line could never carry (a rendered
        7.1.4's twelve slots, say). CI builds the other two and runs neither.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_I2S
    bool "I2S DAC (standard or TDM, reconfigured dynamically)"

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    bool "Capture (convert and check, no peripheral)"

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_NULL
    bool "None (count frames only)"

endchoice

# Which real sink the capture sink is standing in for. An int rather than a
# bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant; see the note on the source choice
# above for why the preprocessor is avoided here.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_CAPTURE_TDM
    int "Capture the TDM conversion (1) or the stereo one (0)"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    default 0

# Whether the capture sink paces a Sendspin stream's timed writes as a DAC
# would, or takes them as fast as they come. An int so it reaches C++ as a
# plain constant.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_CAPTURE_PACED
    int "Pace a Sendspin stream's writes as a DAC would (1) or not (0)"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        0, what CI runs: every decoded sample is written and checked as it
        comes, with no time attached, so no burst is ever late however slowly
        the emulator decodes, and the levels a Sendspin stream leaves are the
        stream's whole. 1: each write waits as a twelve-buffer DMA queue
        would and reports when it would have played, which runs the Sendspin
        player's timing under the emulator's clock.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_TDM_SLOTS
    int "TDM slots the capture sink emulates"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    default 8
    range 2 16
    help
        How many slots the capture sink's emulated TDM frame carries. It has
        no peripheral behind it and so no hardware ceiling, which is exactly
        why CI uses it to check layouts wider than any real line here could
        carry - a rendered 7.1.4's twelve slots, say - rather than the real
        I2S sink, whose own ceiling now comes from AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_
        SLOT_BITS and AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE (ac3forge/sink_plan.hpp)
        and reconfigures itself to whatever a layout needs rather than
        holding to a number fixed here. Slots the layout does not fill are
        still written as zeros rather than skipped, the same rule the real
        sink follows. The layout (AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_LAYOUT) must have no more
        slots than this when the capture sink is what's built.

# WHERE THE BYTES COME FROM. A directory CMake picks, the mirror of the sink
# choice above; see main/byte_source.hpp.
choice AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE
    prompt "Bitstream source"
    default AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_PARTITION
    help
        CI runs three of the four under QEMU: the partition, the FAT volume in
        flash, and HTTP over QEMU's OpenCores Ethernet MAC (see
        AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK). The SD source needs an SDMMC host, which
        QEMU does not have, so CI builds it and runs its file layer
        (main/source/file_common.hpp) through the FAT volume instead. HTTP
        over WiFi is built and not run.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_PARTITION
    bool "Flash partition"

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    bool "SD card (SDMMC + FATFS)"

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_FATFS
    bool "FAT filesystem in flash"

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP
    bool "HTTP over WiFi"

endchoice

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_FATFS_PATH
    string "Path to the bitstream in the flash filesystem"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_FATFS
    default "/audio/sample.ac3"


config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_PATH
    string "Path to the bitstream on the card"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default "/sdcard/sample.ac3"

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_BUS_WIDTH
    int "SDMMC bus width"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 4
    range 1 4
    help
        4-bit if the card is wired for it. 1-bit works on any wiring and is
        several times slower, which matters less for the bitrate (448 kbit/s is
        nothing) than for the CPU time the transfer costs.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_CLK_GPIO
    int "SDMMC CLK GPIO"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 14

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_CMD_GPIO
    int "SDMMC CMD GPIO"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 15

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_D0_GPIO
    int "SDMMC D0 GPIO"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 2

# WHICH NETWORK carries the HTTP source. A directory CMake picks
# (main/source/http/net/), the same rule as the source and sink seams: the HTTP
# client never learns which. WiFi is what a board has. The OpenCores Ethernet
# MAC is what QEMU has - `idf.py qemu` attaches one to the host's network, and
# the guest reaches the host at 10.0.2.2 - so it is how CI runs this source end
# to end with no board and no access point.
choice AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK
    prompt "Network for the HTTP source"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP
    default AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    bool "WiFi station"

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_OPENETH
    bool "OpenCores Ethernet MAC (QEMU only)"
    select ETH_USE_OPENETH
    help
        The MAC qemu-system-xtensa emulates. Not a device that exists on any
        board, which is why the driver refuses to run on one.

endchoice

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_WIFI_SSID
    string "WiFi SSID"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    default ""
    help
        Empty by default, so a board with nothing else stored listens for
        Improv at once (network.hpp) rather than spending the retries and
        the association timeout on a placeholder network first. Set this
        only for a fleet flashed from one image with one network in mind;
        Improv or `PUT /network` is how a single board is told its own.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "WiFi password"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    default ""

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_WIFI_RETRIES
    int "Quick retries before a join is reported as failed"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    default 5
    help
        How many times the station tries a network again at once, before a
        join at boot or over Improv is reported as failed. Bounded, so a wrong
        password is reported instead of the join waiting for good: a player
        that silently never starts is harder to diagnose than one that says it
        could not associate. At the default the report comes about 15 s after
        the first try for a network that is not there, and about 25 s for a
        wrong password.

        The station keeps trying after that, waiting 1, 2, 4 and 8 s and then
        15 s between tries, until it joins or is given another network. A
        network the board loses after joining gets the same treatment: these
        quick retries first, then the waits. A board whose access point
        restarts, or that boots before its access point after a power cut,
        joins once the network is back.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_HTTP_URL
    string "URL of the bitstream"
    depends on AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP
    default "http://example.invalid/sample.ac3"

# THE OUTPUT LAYOUT - src/forge/include/ac3/render/layout.hpp. The
# speakers the room has, one per output slot; the stream is rendered onto them
# whatever it was coded as (ac3/render/render.hpp). A stereo or mono layout is
# the decoder's own §7.8 fold; anything else is placed channel by channel, and
# a layout with height speakers has the stream's objects reconstructed and
# placed by their own positions.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_LAYOUT
    string "Output layout: a name (2.0, 5.1, 7.1.4, 9.2.4) or a speaker list"
    default "2.0"
    help
        A NAME is F.L.H: F speakers on the ring (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7 or 9), L
        low-frequency feeds (0, 1 or 2), H heights (0, 2, 4 or 6), and the
        slots come out ring, heights, LFE in Table E2.5 order - "5.1" is
        L C R Ls Rs LFE, the AC-3 order. A LIST is one token per slot in slot
        order: a location name (L C R Ls Rs Lrs Rrs Lw Rw Vhl Vhr Vhc Lts Rts
        Ts Cs LFE LFE2), azimuth/elevation in degrees ("30/0"; left is
        positive), or "-" for a slot nothing is on. "L,R,C,LFE,Ls,Rs" is a 5.1
        DAC wired in WAV order. The slot count must fit the sink: the real
        I2S sink reconfigures itself to whatever a layout needs, up to its
        ceiling from AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLOT_BITS and AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_
        SECOND_LINE; the capture sink is capped at AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_TDM_SLOTS.
        PUT /layout on the control surface changes it for the next play.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_DRC_MODE
    int "Operating mode: line (0), RF (1), or as coded with no normalisation and no DRC (2)"
    default 0
    range 0 2
    help
        §7.7's two consumer modes, or neither. Line mode normalises to the
        stream's dialnorm and applies the transmitted dynrng words - what a
        player feeding a wide-range system does, and the default. RF mode adds
        compr, applied with 11 dB that put dialogue at -20 dBFS, and overload
        protection for a set-top box. 2 leaves the samples
        as coded, which is what apps/baremetal/probe.cpp's references are
        measured against; sdkconfig.ci-render uses it so the rendered levels
        can be compared with the probe's to the digit.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_STEREO_FOLD
    int "Fold for a 2.0 layout: Lo/Ro (0) or Lt/Rt (1)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        §7.8.1's plain stereo, or §7.8.2's Dolby Surround compatible fold for
        a matrix decoder downstream. Only a two-speaker layout uses this.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_OBJECTS
    int "Object layer: reconstruct when the layout has height (0), never (1), always (2)"
    default 0
    range 0 2
    help
        An Atmos stream's bed is the objects' own 5.1 fold, so a room without
        height speakers loses nothing by playing the bed - and saves about
        10 ms of every 32 ms frame on this part, plus the reconstruction's
        heap (see AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_JOC_DOMAIN). 0 reconstructs exactly when
        the layout has a height speaker; 2 does so for any rendered layout,
        so a 7.1 room gets its rear surrounds from the objects rather than
        from the panned bed; 1 never does. A layout that folds never does.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_JOC_DOMAIN
    int "JOC reconstruction domain: QMF (0) or MDCT band (1)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        Which domain the object reconstruction applies TS 103 420's matrix in.
        0, the 64-band QMF, is what the clause describes and what every stream
        from a licensed encoder needs; it allocates about 233 KB, which on this
        part means PSRAM. 1, the MDCT-band approximation, is correct ONLY for a
        stream this project's own encoder produced with the same setting, and
        allocates about 148 KB - which is how the QEMU shape, with no PSRAM,
        renders the height fixture (sdkconfig.ci-render). Not a choice for a
        board playing real content.

# THE PLAYER'S TWO TASKS - see esp-idf/ac3forge/include/ac3forge/player.hpp. The
# fetch task reads the source into a ring; the decode task drains the ring,
# decodes and writes to the sink. WiFi and TCP/IP run on core 0, so the fetch
# task goes beside them and the decode task has core 1 to itself. -1 lets the
# scheduler choose, for a build with one core.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_FETCH_CORE
    int "Core for the fetch task (-1 = any)"
    default 0
    range -1 1

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_CORE
    int "Core for the decode task (-1 = any)"
    default 1
    range -1 1

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_RING_BYTES
    int "Ring between fetch and decode, bytes of bitstream"
    default 32768
    range 4096 262144
    help
        How long a stall in the source the DAC never hears: 32 KB is 0.57 s of
        a 448 kbit/s stream. In PSRAM when the part has it and the option
        below says so, in internal SRAM otherwise.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_RING_IN_PSRAM
    int "Put the ring in PSRAM when present (1) or internal SRAM (0)"
    default 1
    range 0 1
    help
        An int rather than a bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant, the
        same reason the capture sink's choice is one.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_STACK_BYTES
    int "Stack for the decode task, bytes"
    default 32768
    range 12288 65536
    help
        The player reports what the decode actually used as
        stream.decode_stack_free at the end of a run: this minus that. Measured
        under QEMU on 2026-09-10, an AC-3 5.1 decode left 20,604 of 32,768
        spare. The default keeps the probe's margin; a shape that is short of
        heap can spend some of it, knowing the number.

# A PLAY'S START - PlayerConfig::hold_first_unit. The first access unit held
# until the second has decoded, so the sink starts with two frames queued;
# planning/esp32-714-realtime.md, decision 14, has why 7.1.4 over WiFi wants it.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_HOLD_FIRST_UNIT
    int "Hold a play's first unit until its second has decoded (1) or not (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        A play's first frames decode more slowly than the rest, and with only
        the first frame queued a 7.1.4 stream played over WiFi ran the DAC dry
        in them. Holding the first unit costs 32 ms before a play is heard, and
        a copy of the unit while it is held: 1 KB a channel for each of its six
        blocks, in PSRAM when the part has it.

# THE CONTROL SURFACE - ac3forge::Control, esp-idf/ac3forge/include/ac3forge/
# control.hpp: GET /status, POST /play with a location, POST /stop, POST /volume.
# Only worth having where there is a network, so 0 (off) by default; the HTTP
# source's configurations turn it on.
config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_CONTROL_PORT
    int "TCP port for the REST control surface (0 = none)"
    default 0
    range 0 65535

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_MAX_LAPS
    int "Stop after this many passes through the stream (0 = never stop)"
    default 0
    help
        0 plays forever, which is what a demo on a board should do. CI sets a
        small number so the application finishes and prints a verdict something
        can gate on.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_REPORT_EVERY_FRAMES
    int "Print a progress line every this many frames (0 = only at the end of a pass)"
    default 0
    help
        The timing line is printed at the end of each pass through the stream.
        A source that cannot rewind - HTTP - makes exactly one pass, which for
        a long stream means one line after minutes of silence on the console.
        A non-zero value prints the same figures, cumulative, every N frames as
        well; 125 is every four seconds at 48 kHz. A Sendspin stream prints a
        sendspin.progress line every N chunks, with the internal heap free.

# THE SENDSPIN PLAYER - main/sendspin.hpp, over the component's player
# (CONFIG_AC3FORGE_SENDSPIN, in the component's own menu). A build with a
# network and the component's player has one; each of these sizes it.
menu "Sendspin player"
    depends on AC3FORGE_SENDSPIN

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_RING_BYTES
    int "Ring of chunks between the session and the decode, bytes"
    default 262144
    range 8192 4194304
    help
        What a server may send ahead of playing it, less a little for stream
        starts and ends, is this: 256 KB is about four and a half seconds of
        a 448 kbit/s E-AC-3 stream's bursts, or 0.9 s of 24-bit stereo PCM.
        In PSRAM when the part has it; a build with none (QEMU) sizes it to
        what its internal RAM can spare.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_MAX_CHUNK_BYTES
    int "The largest chunk taken, bytes"
    default 25600
    range 4096 65535
    help
        An E-AC-3 burst at the bitstream's ceiling is 24,585 bytes with its
        header, and 150 ms of 24-bit stereo PCM 21,613. A shape that plays
        only small streams can take less, and the decode task's buffer for
        one chunk shrinks with it.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_DECODE_STACK_BYTES
    int "Stack for the Sendspin decode task, bytes"
    default 32768
    range 12288 65536
    help
        As AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_STACK_BYTES, for the task that decodes a
        Sendspin stream's bursts: the decoder's frames are most of it. The
        page's Sendspin section says what a run left spare.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_SERVER_STACK_BYTES
    int "Stack for the Sendspin WebSocket server's task, bytes"
    default 8192
    range 6144 32768
    help
        Every session runs on this task, the Noise handshake's X25519 and
        SHA-256 included. The page's Sendspin section says what a run left
        spare.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_SERVER_PRIORITY
    int "Priority of the Sendspin WebSocket server's task"
    default 5
    range 1 20
    help
        The decode task runs at 6. On an ESP32-S3 the two are on different
        cores and this one's priority decides little. On a part with one core
        it decides whether a clock reply or a chunk that arrives during a
        burst's decode waits for the burst: below 6 it does, and the reply is
        timestamped late.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_CLOCK_PRIORITY
    int "Priority of the Sendspin server's task while a clock reply is due (0 = unchanged)"
    default 0
    range 0 20
    help
        Above the decode task's 6 only while the playback server's clock
        exchange waits for its reply, for at most 200 ms after it went out
        (SendspinHostConfig::clock_priority). On a part with one core the
        replies are otherwise read when a burst's decode ends, every reply of
        a burst about as late as the others. On an ESP32-C6 playing a stream
        every such burst was left out of the clock, and once thirty had been
        the clock followed the delay and moved 13 to 31 ms. At 7, ten minutes
        kept the clock within 3 ms, but the server task handled the stream's
        chunks above the decode for every clock burst, and 595 blocks
        underran. Off until that work costs less.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_MAX_DELAY_MS
    int "Longest per-output delay a server can set, milliseconds (0 = none)"
    default 20
    range 0 100
    help
        The delay lines are one buffer per output of this length: sixteen
        outputs of 20 ms is about 61 KB, in PSRAM when the part has it.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_LEAD_MS
    int "Lead a stream's first chunk needs, milliseconds (required_lead_time_ms)"
    default 250
    range 50 30000
    help
        From a chunk's arrival to its first sample leaving the port: the
        decoder's one-frame hold, a burst's decode and render, and the DMA
        queue, with room to spare.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_BUFFER_MS
    int "Audio kept buffered while playing, milliseconds (min_buffer_ms)"
    default 300
    range 0 30000

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_UNPAIRED_ACCESS
    int "Let an approved server play without pairing (1) or not (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        pairing.md's unpaired access, which a player offers and a server's
        operator approves. Off by default: a board plays only for servers it
        has paired with.

config AC3FORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_PCM
    int "Offer player@v1 with PCM (1), for Music Assistant, or only _ac3forge_player@v1 (0)"
    default 1
    range 0 1

endmenu

endmenu
